<figure class="merge-walk paper-surface" aria-labelledby="merge-walk-title">
  <figcaption id="merge-walk-title"><strong>Merging orders and order lines</strong><br />A merge join matches the first two orders to their six lines.</figcaption>
  <div class="merge-inputs">
    <div><h3>Orders</h3><p><b>1</b><span>2</span><span>3 …</span></p><small>One row per order ID</small></div>
    <div><h3>Lines’ order IDs</h3><p><b>1 · 1 · 1</b><span>2 · 2 · 2</span><span>3 …</span></p><small>Three lines per ID</small></div>
  </div>
  <ol><li>Match order 1 with each of its three lines, producing three joined rows.</li><li>The next line belongs to order 2. Move to order 2 in the orders table.</li><li>Match order 2 with its three lines, then continue to order 3.</li></ol>
  <p class="merge-note">Each order ID appears once in the orders table, so each line matches one order. If both sides had repeated keys, the join would need to return every matching pair.</p>
</figure>
